---
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// HOMEPAGE LABORATORY — "Editorial Trailhead"  (design experiment, 2026-08-25)
//
// WHAT THIS IS. An isolated prototype testing one hypothesis: that datumwise's front page should
// behave like an editorial trailhead — a curated set of things worth discovering — rather than a
// diagram of the institution. It is NOT the homepage, NOT linked from navigation, and excluded from
// the sitemap. The production homepage is untouched.
//
// THE COMPOSITION, AND WHY IT IS SHAPED LIKE THIS.
// The brief's real danger is three disguised cards: removing the pillar labels does not remove the
// pillar grid. The defence is not restraint, it is ASYMMETRY — of size, form, register, and dwell
// time. So this page is a SEQUENCE with one dominant story and four unequal encounters after it:
//
//   1. THE LEAD (huge, interactive, ~2 min)  — you pick a denominator and discover your answer has
//      rivals of equal standing. One gesture, one aha. This is the only place the page asks for real
//      participation, and it is deliberately the first thing, before any vocabulary.
//   2. A MARGINAL QUESTION (tiny, ~20 sec)   — Evidence, as a strange small joke at a border post.
//   3. THE MACHINE (dark, interrupting)      — the paper register breaks and real generated wire
//      appears. Small: ONE governed refusal, not a four-mood exhibit.
//   4. A DIALOGUE (medium, quiet)            — Intelligence, as an exchange with nothing to run yet.
//   5. THE SHELF (calm, serious)             — one publication, properly, plus the doors.
//   6. THE LANDSCAPE (small, late)           — the only place the three questions are named.
//
// Nothing here is a card, and no two objects share a container, a rhythm, or a type scale.
//
// WHAT HOLDS IT TOGETHER, given all that: one editorial voice that visibly SELECTS ("what is worth
// seeing today"), one paper ground broken exactly once, and one throughline — every object on this
// page is about the gap between a number being correct and a number being usable.
//
// NO-JS. Everything works with JavaScript off. The lead is `<input type=radio>` + `:checked ~`; the
// rest is static composition. This page ships zero JavaScript of its own.
//
// HONESTY CONSTRAINTS.
//  · The machine block renders GENERATED wire via lib/exhibitA.ts, which re-adjudicates at build
//    time and throws if the shipped package stopped behaving that way. No hand-typed wire.
//  · Evidence and Intelligence have NO executable surface and are presented as paper only. Neither
//    block implies a runtime, and the Intelligence one says outright that nothing runs yet.
//  · Every publication fact derives from data/publications.ts. Zero literal Zenodo tokens here.
//  · The lead's arithmetic is a worked teaching scenario, fully shown so a reader can check it — not
//    a claim about the engine, and it does not pretend to be output.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { citation } from '../../data/publications';
import { CASES, PROVENANCE } from '../../lib/exhibitA';

const theory = citation('w-theory-of-data');

// ONE governed refusal, from the generated transcript. Not the four-mood exhibit — a single
// encounter, because the editorial point is "the machine can decline", not "here is our feature".
const refusal = CASES.find((c) => c.mood === 'refuse');
if (!refusal) throw new Error('trailhead: the generated transcript carries no refuse case');

// THE LEAD'S SCENARIO. The arithmetic is shown in full on the page so the reader can check every
// number; nothing is asserted that the visible figures do not support.
//   52 shops on the roster · 3 shut for refurbishment → 49 open · 8 of those had a dead till feed
//   → 41 reported · 6 of the reporting shops sold an umbrella.
const READINGS = [
  { k: 'reported', d: 41, label: 'shops that reported that day', pct: '14.6' },
  { k: 'open',     d: 49, label: 'shops open for business',      pct: '12.2' },
  { k: 'roster',   d: 52, label: 'every shop on the roster',     pct: '11.5' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Trailhead — a homepage laboratory"
  description="A laboratory prototype: what if the front page were a curated set of things worth discovering rather than a diagram of the institution?"
  wide={true}
  chip={false}
>

  {/* ── DATELINE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* Not a hero. A dateline — the editorial promise that someone CHOSE these things, which is the
      whole hypothesis. It is also where the experiment declares itself. */}
  <header class="dateline">
    <p class="dl-l mono">datumwise · laboratory prototype · not the homepage</p>
    <p class="dl-r mono">what is worth seeing today</p>
  </header>

  {/* ── 1 · THE LEAD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* The dominant story. No vocabulary, no institution, no pillars — a question anyone who has ever
      computed a percentage can answer, and an answer that turns out to have rivals. */}
  <section class="lead" aria-labelledby="lead-h">
    <p class="kicker mono">the thing worth seeing today</p>
    <h1 id="lead-h" class="lead-h">
      Six shops sold an umbrella.<br />What fraction of your shops is that?
    </h1>

    <div class="scene">
      <p class="scene-p">
        A region of <strong>52 shops</strong>, last Tuesday. Three were shut for refurbishment.
        Of the 49 that were open, eight had a dead till feed and reported nothing at all. Of the
        41 that did report, <strong>six</strong> sold at least one umbrella.
      </p>
      <p class="scene-p scene-ask">
        Everything above is true and undisputed. Pick your denominator.
      </p>
    </div>

    <form class="ballot">
      <fieldset>
        <legend class="visually-hidden">Which shops belong in the denominator?</legend>
        {READINGS.map((r) => (
          <>
            <input class="b-in" type="radio" name="denom" id={`d-${r.k}`} />
            <label class="b-opt" for={`d-${r.k}`}>
              <span class="b-n mono">{r.d}</span>
              <span class="b-t">{r.label}</span>
            </label>
          </>
        ))}

        {/* Revealed by any commitment. The point is not your number — it is that the other two
            arrive beside it at the same size, and none of them is a mistake.
            IT LIVES INSIDE THE FIELDSET DELIBERATELY: `~` only reaches SIBLINGS, and the first cut
            had the inputs in the fieldset and this block outside it, so the reveal silently never
            fired while the selected option still highlighted — the most convincing kind of broken. */}
        <div class="verdict" role="note">
        <p class="v-cap mono">all three, at the same size, because that is the situation</p>
        <ol class="v-row">
          {READINGS.map((r) => (
            <li class="v-cell" data-k={r.k}>
              <span class="v-pct">{r.pct}<span class="v-sign">%</span></span>
              <span class="v-frac mono">6 ÷ {r.d}</span>
              <span class="v-lab">{r.label}</span>
              <span class="v-mine mono">← the one you picked</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
        <p class="v-after">
          Not one of these is an error. Each answers a slightly different question, every one of them
          is a question somebody in the building genuinely means, and nothing in the data says which
          one was asked. Two of them will be in the same deck by Thursday.
        </p>
        <p class="v-after v-sting">
          And the numerator is worse. Six is what the reporting shops reported. The eight silent
          shops did not sell zero umbrellas — they said <em>nothing</em>, which is a different thing
          that arithmetic cannot tell apart from zero. <strong>Six is a floor, not a count.</strong>
        </p>
          <p class="v-exit">
            There is a four-minute walk that starts one step earlier than this — at a single number
            with nothing attached to it at all. <a href="/park/when-is-it-data">When is it data?</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </fieldset>
    </form>
  </section>

  {/* ── 2 · THE MARGINAL QUESTION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* Evidence, deliberately TINY. The page's second object is one-twentieth the size of its first,
      which is the strongest single defence against the three-card layout. It is also a joke, and the
      joke is the actual open problem. It stays paper: nothing here implies a runtime, because for
      Evidence there is not one. */}
  <aside class="margin-note" aria-labelledby="mn-h">
    <p class="mn-stamp mono">a small question we keep having</p>
    <h2 id="mn-h" class="mn-h">Probability: purpose of visit?</h2>
    <p class="mn-p">
      It arrives at the border with the confident air of something that belongs everywhere. Asked to
      state its business, it gives three different answers depending on who is behind the desk: a
      property of the world, a state of somebody's knowledge, or the long-run behaviour of a
      procedure nobody intends to repeat.
    </p>
    <p class="mn-p">
      All three are waved through. They are not the same traveller, they do not license the same
      conclusions, and the number on the passport is identical in every case.
    </p>
    <p class="mn-more">
      <a href="/evidence/walkthrough">The evidence, walked</a> — where this stops being funny.
    </p>
  </aside>

  {/* ── 3 · THE MACHINE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* The one place the paper register breaks. It is an INTERRUPTION, not a product section: a single
      real refusal, generated by running the shipped package at build time, encountered the way you
      would encounter a working instrument in a room full of arguments.
      It is deliberately NOT the four-mood exhibit. The editorial claim is "something here can say
      no", and four tabs would turn that into a feature tour. */}
  <section class="machine ds ds-wire ds-crossing" aria-labelledby="mach-h">
    <div class="mach-in">
      <p class="mach-kick mono">an interruption</p>
      <h2 id="mach-h" class="mach-h">Some questions deserve an answer.</h2>
      <p class="mach-sub">Some don't, and something should be willing to say so out loud.</p>

      <div class="wire">
        <p class="wire-lab mono">asked</p>
        <pre class="wire-req mono">{refusal.request}</pre>

        <p class="wire-lab mono">answered</p>
        <p class="wire-verdict mono">{refusal.mood}</p>

        {refusal.findings.map((f) => (
          <>
            <p class="wire-code mono">{f.code}</p>
            <p class="wire-detail">{f.detail}</p>
          </>
        ))}

        {refusal.noResult && (
          <>
            <p class="wire-lab mono">instead</p>
            <ul class="wire-offers">
              {refusal.noResult.offers.map((o) => <li>{o}</li>)}
            </ul>
          </>
        )}
      </div>

      <p class="mach-prov mono">
        manifold {PROVENANCE.manifold} · columna {PROVENANCE.columna} · contract {PROVENANCE.contract}
        — produced by running the shipped package at build time, not written by hand
      </p>
      <p class="mach-after">
        Refusing is not the software failing. It is the only honest thing left when the question has
        no lawful answer, and it is startlingly hard to buy.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── 4 · THE DIALOGUE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* Intelligence: the youngest ground, and the form admits it. A dialogue rather than an exhibit,
      because there is nothing to exhibit — and saying so plainly is more interesting than dressing
      an open research question as a capability. */}
  <section class="dialogue" aria-labelledby="dlg-h">
    <p class="dlg-kick mono">overheard, roughly</p>
    <h2 id="dlg-h" class="dlg-h">It passed every test. Now what?</h2>

    <dl class="script">
      <dt class="who mono">the model</dt>
      <dd class="says">The number is correct. I checked it four ways.</dd>

      <dt class="who mono">the analyst</dt>
      <dd class="says">I believe you.</dd>

      <dt class="who mono">the model</dt>
      <dd class="says">So I have gone ahead and repriced the region.</dd>

      <dt class="who mono">the analyst</dt>
      <dd class="says">You have done <em>what</em>?</dd>
    </dl>

    <p class="dlg-after">
      Nothing in that exchange was a mistake about arithmetic. Being right is a claim about the world.
      Being allowed is a claim about who decided, on what authority, and whether anyone could have
      stopped it. A correct answer carries no permission with it, and we have no good habit — legal,
      technical, or social — for saying where the second thing comes from.
    </p>
    <p class="dlg-frontier mono">
      This is the newest ground here and the least built. There is no runtime behind this paragraph,
      and we would rather say so than draw you a diagram of one.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ── 5 · THE SHELF ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* The calm serious moment. ONE publication, given properly — not a row of citation cards — plus
      the standing doors, so a researcher, architect or engineer can leave for the real material at
      any point without completing a single playful thing. Every fact derived. */}
  <section class="shelf" aria-labelledby="shelf-h">
    <p class="shelf-kick mono">behind all of this</p>
    <h2 id="shelf-h" class="shelf-h">There are full arguments, with proofs.</h2>
    <p class="shelf-lede">
      The walks are on-ramps. They are deliberately smaller than the work, and they simplify in
      places a serious reader will want undone.
    </p>

    <div class="feature">
      <a class="feat-title" href={theory.href}>{theory.label}</a>
      <p class="feat-meta mono">
        {theory.versionTag ? `${theory.versionTag} · ` : ''}{theory.date} · deposited
      </p>
      <p class="feat-blurb">
        The foundation: what an analytical object is, where quantities live, which combinations are
        lawful, and why a growing fragment of it is machine-checked rather than asserted.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="doors">
      <li><a href="/research">The research corpus</a><span>every paper, one preserved map</span></li>
      <li><a href="/analytical-governance">Analytical Governance</a><span>the serving and authority boundary</span></li>
      <li><a href="/columna">Columna</a><span>the machine, and what it actually does</span></li>
      <li><a href="/case">The Case</a><span>one warehouse, worked end to end</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  {/* ── 6 · THE LANDSCAPE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* The ONLY place the three questions are named, and the last thing on the page. It is small on
      purpose: after four encounters the reader has the terrain, and the sentence only has to point
      at what they already walked. If it opened the page it would be a taxonomy to memorise. Here it
      is a recognition. */}
  <section class="landscape" aria-labelledby="land-h">
    <hr class="land-rule" />
    <h2 id="land-h" class="land-h">You were in three different places just now.</h2>
    <p class="land-p">
      The umbrellas were an argument about <em>what a number is about</em>. The traveller at the
      border was an argument about <em>what a number supports</em>. The repriced region was an
      argument about <em>what may then be done</em>. They look like one subject from a distance and
      they come apart the moment you need any of them to hold.
    </p>
    <p class="land-p land-quiet">
      Being right about the first buys you nothing about the second, and being right about both buys
      you nothing about the third. Most of the damage happens in the two gaps.
    </p>
    <p class="land-foot mono">
      That is the whole map, and it is the last thing on this page rather than the first on purpose.
    </p>
  </section>

  <style>
    /* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       LABORATORY STYLES, scoped to this page. They borrow the site's TOKENS (paper, ink, rules,
       wire ramp, fonts) and none of its components — the experiment is precisely about whether
       objects may differ, so a shared component costume would decide the question in advance.
       Six objects, six rhythms, six measures, no repeated container.
       ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    .mono { font-family: var(--font-mono); }

    /* ── dateline ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    .dateline {
      display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; flex-wrap: wrap;
      width: min(100% - 3rem, 68rem); margin: 2.5rem auto 0; padding-bottom: 0.7rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink);
      font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft);
    }
    .dl-r { color: var(--ink); }

    /* ── 1 · the lead — the page's only wide, loud object ──────────────────────────────────── */
    .lead { width: min(100% - 3rem, 60rem); margin-inline: auto; padding-block: clamp(3rem, 2rem + 5vw, 6rem) 0; }
    .kicker { font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
    .lead-h {
      font-family: var(--font-serif);
      font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 1.2rem + 4.6vw, 4.6rem);
      line-height: 1.04; letter-spacing: -0.02em; margin: 0 0 2.5rem; max-width: 20ch;
    }
    .scene { max-width: 34rem; }
    .scene-p { font-size: 1.06rem; line-height: 1.62; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
    .scene-ask { color: var(--ink-soft); }

    .ballot { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
    .ballot fieldset { border: 0; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: grid; gap: 0; }
    .b-in {
      position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; margin: -1px;
      overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap;
    }
    /* Ballot lines, not buttons and not the park's list: a number, a rule, a description. */
    .b-opt {
      display: grid; grid-template-columns: 5rem minmax(0, 1fr); align-items: baseline; gap: 1.25rem;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: 1.1rem 0; cursor: pointer;
      transition: border-color 0.15s ease;
    }
    .b-opt:last-of-type { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
    .b-n { font-size: 1.7rem; color: var(--ink-soft); transition: color 0.15s ease; }
    .b-t { font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.4; }
    .b-opt:hover { border-color: var(--ink); }
    .b-opt:hover .b-n { color: var(--ink); }
    .b-in:checked + .b-opt { border-color: var(--ink); }
    .b-in:checked + .b-opt .b-n { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
    .b-in:focus-visible + .b-opt { outline: 2px solid var(--ds-accent, #7a5c2e); outline-offset: 3px; }

    /* Closed until you commit — and closed to assistive tech too, not merely clipped, or the answer
       is announced before the question has been answered. */
    .verdict {
      max-height: 0; opacity: 0; overflow: hidden; visibility: hidden;
      transition: max-height 0.5s ease, opacity 0.4s ease;
    }
    .b-in:checked ~ .verdict {
      max-height: 80rem; opacity: 1; visibility: visible; margin-top: 2.5rem;
    }
    .v-cap { font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 1.25rem; }
    .v-row { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 2rem; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 1.5rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .v-cell { display: grid; gap: 0.3rem; border-top: 2px solid var(--rule-strong); padding-top: 0.9rem; }
    .v-pct { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(2rem, 1.4rem + 2.4vw, 3.2rem); line-height: 1; }
    .v-sign { font-size: 0.45em; margin-left: 0.1em; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .v-frac { font-size: 0.76rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .v-lab { font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.4; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    /* The marker for YOUR pick — set by the sibling radios, one rule each. */
    .v-mine { font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink); visibility: hidden; }
    #d-reported:checked ~ .verdict .v-cell[data-k='reported'],
    #d-open:checked     ~ .verdict .v-cell[data-k='open'],
    #d-roster:checked   ~ .verdict .v-cell[data-k='roster'] { border-top-color: var(--ink); }
    #d-reported:checked ~ .verdict .v-cell[data-k='reported'] .v-mine,
    #d-open:checked     ~ .verdict .v-cell[data-k='open'] .v-mine,
    #d-roster:checked   ~ .verdict .v-cell[data-k='roster'] .v-mine { visibility: visible; }

    .v-after { max-width: 34rem; font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.62; margin: 0 0 1.1rem; }
    .v-sting { border-left: 2px solid var(--ink); padding-left: 1rem; }
    .v-exit { max-width: 34rem; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 1.75rem 0 0; }

    /* ── 2 · the marginal question — one twentieth the size of the lead ────────────────────── */
    .margin-note {
      width: min(100% - 3rem, 60rem); margin-inline: auto;
      padding-block: clamp(4rem, 3rem + 6vw, 8rem);
      display: grid; justify-items: end;
    }
    .margin-note > * { max-width: 26rem; }
    .mn-stamp { font-size: 0.66rem; letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 0.6rem; }
    .mn-h { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 400; line-height: 1.35; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
    .mn-p { font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 0.85rem; }
    .mn-more { font-size: 0.85rem; margin: 0.5rem 0 0; }

    /* ── 3 · the machine — the one break in the paper ground ───────────────────────────────── */
    .machine { background: var(--wire-bg); color: var(--wire-ink); padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 2.5rem + 5vw, 6rem); }
    .mach-in { width: min(100% - 3rem, 46rem); margin-inline: auto; }
    .mach-kick { font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--wire-ink-soft); margin: 0 0 1.25rem; }
    .mach-h { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 1.3rem + 2.2vw, 2.8rem); line-height: 1.15; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
    .mach-sub { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--wire-ink-soft); margin: 0 0 2.5rem; max-width: 32rem; }

    .wire { border-top: 1px solid var(--wire-rule); padding-top: 1.5rem; display: grid; gap: 0.4rem; }
    .wire-lab { font-size: 0.66rem; letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--wire-ink-soft); margin: 1rem 0 0.3rem; }
    .wire-lab:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
    .wire-req {
      font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0; color: var(--wire-key); background: var(--wire-panel);
      padding: 0.8rem 1rem; overflow-x: auto; white-space: pre-wrap; word-break: break-word;
    }
    .wire-verdict {
      font-size: 1.5rem; letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase; margin: 0;
      color: var(--ds-accent-wire, #c9a86a);
    }
    .wire-code { font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--wire-string); margin: 0.9rem 0 0.25rem; }
    .wire-detail { font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.55; margin: 0; color: var(--wire-ink); }
    .wire-offers { margin: 0.4rem 0 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; display: grid; gap: 0.35rem; }
    .wire-offers li { font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--wire-ink-soft); }
    .mach-prov { font-size: 0.68rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--wire-ink-soft); margin: 2rem 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--wire-rule); padding-top: 0.9rem; }
    .mach-after { font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.6; margin: 1.75rem 0 0; max-width: 32rem; }

    /* ── 4 · the dialogue ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    .dialogue { width: min(100% - 3rem, 40rem); margin-inline: auto; padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 2.5rem + 5vw, 7rem); }
    .dlg-kick { font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 0.9rem; }
    .dlg-h { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 1.2rem + 1.8vw, 2.3rem); line-height: 1.2; margin: 0 0 2rem; }
    .script { margin: 0 0 2rem; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 6.5rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 0.85rem 1.25rem; }
    .who { font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft); padding-top: 0.32rem; }
    .says { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.45; }
    .dlg-after { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; }
